  "worked_solution": "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5-y/2} & =\\sqrt[5]{7} \\\\ ln\u2061({e}^{5-y/2}) & =ln\u2061(\\sqrt[5]{7}) \\\\ 5-\\frac{y}{2} & =ln\u2061({7}^{1/5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-\\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}ln\u2061(7) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}ln\u2061(7)-5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{5}ln\u2061(7)+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\ny\u22489.222\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
